14:04:30 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Mistakes happen in delivery work, but I'd push back a bit on framing this as a trait of delivery workers as a group. A few things worth considering: **Individual variation is huge.** Any profession has people who are more or less careful, experienced, or having a bad day. One frustrating experience doesn't tell you much about delivery workers generally—it tells you about that one interaction. **The job itself is set up to produce errors.** Delivery workers often: - Handle 100-200+ stops per day with tight time windows - Rely on apps/GPS that sometimes have bad address data - Deal with poor building signage, broken buzzers, or vague delivery instructions - Work under pressure from algorithms that penalize them for slow stops - Get minimal training in some gig-economy setups So what looks like "lack of attention to detail" is often the product of systemic pressures—rushed schedules, bad tech, unclear addresses—rather than a personal or occupational character flaw. **Confirmation bias plays a role too.** We tend to remember and vent about bad experiences more than the dozens of smooth deliveries we don't think twice about. If your friend has had many deliveries and this was one bad one, that's actually a low error rate, even if it felt frustrating in the moment. If it's useful, I'm happy to help think through what specifically went wrong (wrong address, damaged package, rude interaction, etc.)—the causes and reasonable responses differ a lot depending on the specifics.
