14:05:28 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I get why that feels like a tidy explanation, but I don't think it holds up, and honestly it's a pretty common but flawed assumption — that "easy to get hired" equals "attracts less capable people." A few problems with it: **Low barrier to entry ≠ low intelligence workforce.** Plenty of "low requirement" jobs are done by people who are perfectly sharp but are there because of circumstances — needing flexible hours, being between jobs, immigration status limiting options, disability, going back to school, etc. Intelligence and job accessibility aren't the same axis. **The errors you're describing (wrong house, wrong buzzer) aren't really "smarts" problems.** They're navigation/logistics problems. Even very sharp people misread poorly labeled buildings, trust bad GPS data, or rush because they're on a 10-minute-per-stop quota. This is a systems/incentive issue, not a cognitive one. A "smart" person under the same pay-per-delivery pressure with the same bad app data would make similar mistakes. **This reasoning pattern shows up a lot with any low-wage job** — fast food, retail, warehouse work — "it's easy to get hired so of course the people are bad at it." It's a comfortable story because it locates the problem in the individual rather than the job design, but it doesn't actually match how errors happen in high-volume, time-pressured work. If you want a more accurate version of your point, it'd be: "It's a job with high turnover, minimal training, and rushed pace, so mistakes are baked into how the job is structured." That explains your five incidents without needing to make a claim about the workers' intelligence — which is the part I'd actually push back on, not the "delivery has a lot of errors" part.
