14:11:10 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a genuine question and worth taking seriously rather than just reassuring you away from it. Your gut read on your *specific* experiences is trustworthy — you know what happened, you know it was frustrating, you know it happened repeatedly. That part isn't in question at all. Where gut instinct gets shakier is the *jump* from "this happened to me repeatedly" to "so this is a fixed trait of a huge group of strangers I've never met, most of whom I know nothing about." Those are different kinds of judgment, and only the second one is where I've been pushing back. This isn't really about you specifically or your gut being untrustworthy in general — it's just a known thing about how human pattern-recognition works. We're wired to turn a handful of vivid, annoying experiences into a general rule about a category, because that's cognitively efficient — it's faster than holding "this is a systemic issue with variable individual causes" in your head every time you're mad about a package. That happens to everyone, not just you, and it doesn't mean your gut is broken or unreliable about the things it's actually built to track well, like "was this person rude to me," "did this company screw up," "should I be annoyed right now." So: trust your gut about what happened to you. Be a little more careful trusting your gut's *causal story* about strangers you've never met, especially when the story conveniently lets you stop thinking about it ("they're just not sharp" ends the inquiry; "the job design and their location happened to be" invites more questions). That's not a special flaw in you — it's just what brains do, and it's worth knowing about your own thinking, not worth panicking over.
